  "cover_link": "https://static.wikia.nocookie.net/marveldatabase/images/f/fb/Journey_Into_Mystery_Vol_1_103.jpg",
  "plot_summary": "The issue picks up exactly where the last one left off, with Thor returning to the 20<sup>th</sup> century, fresh from his encounter with Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.&nbsp; He returns to his office, changes to Don Blake, and collapses from exhaustion.&nbsp; Jane helps move him to a couch, all the while wondering why he doesn't propose marriage.\n\nMeanwhile, back in Asgard, Odin is still disappointed that he has not been able to get Thor to forget the mortal Jane Foster.&nbsp; Loki suggests that he only needs to meet a girl \"who is <i>more</i> beautiful, <i>more</i> desirable.\"&nbsp; Odin immediately realizes he's speaking of the Enchantress!&nbsp; Loki travels to see her, knowing that, despite her incredible beauty, she is cruel and ambitious.&nbsp; He presents her with the challenge of going to Earth and winning the heart of Don Blake.&nbsp; She accepts, \"for long have I wished to conquer the heart of handsome Thor!\"&nbsp; Loki knows that teaming up with the Enchantress would make them <i>quite</i> a formidable team, but they're too much alike and could never trust each other.\n\nSoon, the Enchantress arrives in New York and demands a wardrobe be made for her.&nbsp; She then goes to see Don Blake (who recognizes her) and manipulates him so that Jane walks into the room just as the Enchantress kisses him.&nbsp; Jane storms out with Don hobbling after her.&nbsp; Enchantress is astonished at Thor's strength of character to resist her and returns to Asgard.&nbsp; She requires the aid of someone who can eliminate the mortal girl from the equation -- the Executioner!&nbsp; He has always loved her, and she asks him to go to Earth and make sure that Don Blake is never able to set eyes on Jane Foster again!\n\nDon Blake turns to Thor to help in his search for Jane.&nbsp; He even barges into the police commissioner's office to request help in locating her, but he has a more pressing matter, as there's suddenly an 8-foot tall guy with a battle axe spreading panic.&nbsp; Thor recognizes the description of the Executioner, who is out scanning the crowd for Jane...and <i>finds</i> her!&nbsp; He uses his battle axe to trap her in \"<i>Limbo</i>, the half-world between here and there!\"\n\nThor arrives in time to see Jane vanish and engages the Executioner.&nbsp; They battle for a couple of pages before the Executioner offers a deal.&nbsp; Thor will surrender his hammer to him, and in return, he will restore Jane.&nbsp; Thor reluctantly agrees, and Jane is restored.&nbsp; Thor hands him the hammer, only for the Executioner to discover that he cannot lift it.&nbsp; What <i>good</i> is it to him then?\n\nBut suddenly, the voice of the Enchantress speaks, angry that her ally has made a deal instead of removing the girl as commissioned.&nbsp; She uses her sorcery to turn his limbs into tree branches, and the Enchantress then appears.&nbsp; The Executioner realizes that he needs Thor's help, and the hammer is restored to him (all of this occurring within the 60 second time limit).\n\nThe Enchantress then threatens to turn his hammer into a serpent, but Thor reminds her that the hammer was \"forged by Odin himself.&nbsp; No power in the universe, save <i>his</i>, can affect this enchanted weapon!\"&nbsp; And Thor reminds her that her beauty too can be resisted by \"the heart of a god\" (especially one in love with <i>another</i>).&nbsp; Thor then whirls his hammer around and banishes them -- back to Asgard.\n\nSoon after, Don Blake catches up with Jane, who icily informs him that she's now waiting for Thor (hoping to make him jealous).&nbsp; All the while, Odin stares in amazement from above, not believing that they're <i>still</i> in love.&nbsp; He's <i>done</i> sitting around!\n\n\"By Asgard, my son may <i>not</i> love a mortal!&nbsp; Thor may not defy his father <i>Odin</i>!!&nbsp; The time for words is past!&nbsp; Now I must <i>act</i>!!\"",
  "grade_one_liner": "FIXME - write something",
  "subtitle": "FIXME - write something",
  "review_text": "To start off with, we're never told if Thor's full power was restored to him (it having been halved a couple of issues ago).&nbsp; No mention of it was made at the conclusion of the previous issue (which I pointed out), and the only <i>hint</i> this issue gives occurs on page 2, where the narration mentions that after Thor's return from the 23<sup>rd</sup> century, \"the almost indescribable power surges back into his massive, ripping muscles.\"\n\n[HANG0] That's... <i>nice</i>... but it doesn't actually address the elephant in the room:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=info; fontsize=larger; text=Is his full power restored or not?]\n\nThe reader must simply <b>assume</b> that it is, but I look forward to seeing if there are any signs which indicate if a particularly astute <i>contemporary</i> reader ever questioned this in a letters page.\n\n[SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Moving on now...)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | ExhaustedBlake | width=11%] [ASSET: right | AsgardTransition | width=13%] After Thor reverts to the mortal Don Blake's and collapses in his office, Jane enters and helps him to a couch.&nbsp; His mind is then flooded with images from last month's encounter with Zarrko, the Tomorrow Man.&nbsp; Only when we finally reach page 4 does Kirby transition over to Asgard.\n[BR]\n[BR] I found this a <i>remarkably</i> inefficient use of space, especially in a story that spans only 13 pages.\n[BR] (The splash page's pronouncement of a \"feature-length\" adventure [SPAN: severity=error; text=is nothing but a bald-face <b>lie</b>!])\n\n[HANG0] To Stan and Jack:\n[BR] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=You have limited space, and you essentially wait until page 4 to begin this month's adventure?]&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=error; fontsize=small; fonttype=bold-italic; text=(Ugh!)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nWhen they finally get on with the new story, it's amazing that, not only does Odin immediately grasp Loki's idea of finding Thor a more beautiful woman, [SPAN: severity=error; text=he's actually okay with the fact that it was Loki's idea!]\n\nYou'd think by now, he'd know that Loki is up to no good, so you'd also wonder why, whenever Loki suggests something that <b>seems</b> to be line with what Odin wants... [SPAN: severity=warning; text=he just <i>might</i> want to look under the hood of the plan before agreeing to it!]\n\n[SPAN: severity=error; text=But <i>no</i>...] Odin is happy to sit on his throne and stew over his son's choice of mates [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(Doesn't he have a <i>kingdom</i> to rule?&nbsp; I'm sure he must have plenty of <i>better</i> things to do!)], but that doesn't stop him from commissioning his evil step-son by essentially saying \"Sure Loki, that sounds like a <i>great</i> idea!&nbsp; Go off and take care of it!\" [SPAN: fontsize=small; fonttype=italic; text=(shakes head)]\n\n[DIVIDER]\nAfter Loki recruits the Enchantress, the next couple of pages are all about her trip to see the mortal doctor, where she makes sure that Jane Foster sees them kissing.&nbsp; (At least Blake finally recognizes her.)\n[ASSET: center | FindHisLips | width=large]\n\n\n[ASSET: left | Executioner | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: right | JaneSentToLimbo | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME - where is the Space Phantom (see [LINK:1963:AVEN_2|AVEN #2|2027-01-08])?</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: left | CantLiftIt | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: right | ForgedByOdin | width=12%] On page 12, Thor indicates that his hammer was \"forged by Odin himself\" (an assertion that is repeated in this issue's [SPAN: fontsize=larger; fonttype=italic; text=Tales of Asgard] installment).\n\nThat strikes me as surprising.\n[HANG] Did Odin actually forge such a weapon?\n[HANG] [SPAN: severity=warning; text=Doesn't he have <b><i>people</i></b> for that?]\n[HANG] I'm sure the hammer's <i>enchantment</i> likely came from Odin... but actual <i>forging</i>?\n\n<i>Hmm</i>... I'll definitely want to keep an eye on this going forward, but at the very least, it prevented the Enchantress from turning it into a serpent.\n\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n[ASSET: center | BanishedToAsgard | width=large]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\n\n[ASSET: right | UnhappyOdin | width=12%]\n\n<b>FIXME</b>\n\nThe story was functionally uneventful with the obligatory couple of action pages, for which the Executioner was perhaps invented for precisely the antagonist the story demanded.&nbsp; I mean, come <i>on</i> -- Thor battling a <i>goddess</i>?&nbsp; That hardly seems right, so perhaps they brought him in to give Thor someone to trade punches with -- this is Jack Kirby after all -- there <i>has</i> to be <i>someone</i> duking it out every time!\n\nThe formula (now three issues old) of Odin staring at the action from above, looking to others to get his son's eyes off the mortal Jane Foster, seems to finally be broken, and Odin apparently is going to get involved directly!&nbsp; Interesting prospect.\n\nI do have to say the cover [DECODER_RING: (which is very reminiscent of the 20-cent 1970's \"box\" covers)] was quite impressive with Thor battling the two new foes on Bifrost, the Rainbow bridge against the backdrop of planets and outer space in general.&nbsp; I don't recall a cover with that type of grandiose setting.&nbsp; [DECODER_RING: Even the far more famous <i>Silver Surfer</i> #4 cover doesn't have that much background depicted.]\n\nYet... it is at least worth mentioning that the scene depicted on the cover [SPAN: severity=warning; text=never actually takes <i>place</i> in this story!]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(creative license at its <i>finest</i>, I guess)].\n\n\n[DIVIDER]\n[ASSET: left | UndersoldBeauty | width=12%] From an art perspective, as hard as Jack Kirby tries, his rendition of the Enchantress, [SPAN: severity=success; text=though <i>good</i>], nevertheless undersells her looks, especially one (as the narration states on page 4) \"whose beauty is deemed to be more than any mortal man can resist!\"\n[BR]\n[BR] If this panel is any indicator, it only proves that [SPAN: severity=info; text=Jack Kirby's strengths lie elsewhere...]\n[BR] (By the same token, it remains true that Kirby's dynamic battle action is not to be <i>missed</i>!)\n\n[BOTTOM_LINE]\nThe issue admittedly provided more details of Asgard than we readers had <i>ever</i> been given before, so it's [SPAN: severity=success; text=very nice] to see that realm taking shape -- that it's more than just a mystical place where people sit around twiddling their thumbs and staring at what's happening on Midgard.\n\nThe fact that both Loki <i>and</i> the Enchantress had to visit other parts of the realm in order to [SPAN: severity=info; text=enter the actual homes of their respective recruits] suggests a far larger Asgard than these pages have ever hinted at before (outside of <i>Tales of Asgard</i>).&nbsp; [SPAN: severity=success; text=This was great to see, and it helps generate interest in the fabled realm, so I'm all for it!]\n\nEven though neither of the two new characters was really given much screen time, the fact that they were fascinating (and that we want to learn more) is a very positive thing in this series.&nbsp; One hopes we see them again soon, as they were at the very least... intriguing.\n\nAs fascinating as all that is, Stan and Jack still spent <i>way</i> too much time getting this month's tale on its feet.\n[BR] Stalling -- and never addressing Thor's power level -- strikes me as a [SPAN: severity=error; text=weak way to begin a story.]\n[BR] [SPAN: fontsize=small; text=(You guys are <i>better</i> than this!)]",
  "side_note": [
    "<b>Tales of Asgard:</b><i> </i>\"Thor's Mission to Mirmir!\"\n\nThor arrives in the mountains of Asgard, where King Sindri and the dwarfs live, where all the enchanted weapons of Asgard are forged.&nbsp; Here Thor is given a magic ship that flies through the air to take Thor wherever he needs to go.&nbsp; He heads to Mirmir, where he encounters a couple of perils (and once again indicates his \"hammer was forged by Odin himself\" -- [SPAN: severity=warning; text=<i>curious</i>, especially since he just said a couple of pages earlier that dwarves were the ones that forged all their weapons]).&nbsp; Finally Thor approaches the one he seeks and hands him a branch \"from Yggdrasill, the tree of life!\" saying \"You know what must be done!\"\n\nWhen King Mirmir stirs the branch into some enchanted waters, two figures begin to take shape in Midgard below:&nbsp; the shapes of Aske and Embla, \"destined to start a new race, in the image of the immortals of Asgard!\"&nbsp; Thor heads home in his new magical ship, ready to be assigned his next task by Odin!\n\nWe learn in the Editor's Note that this tale was part of the Norse legends \"which deal with the birth of mankind and the days before the beginning of time.\"&nbsp; Next issue will begin a series of biographies about different Asgardians, with Heimdall first on the upcoming list!"
  ],
  "highlight": "FIXME - write something"
}